\description{
Quantify source-G-Study sampling uncertainty in relative \code{G}, absolute
dependability \code{Phi}, or measurement \code{SEM} for prespecified future facet
counts. Future counts do not add source degrees of freedom. Inspect
\code{Estimate}/\code{PointStatus} separately from \code{Lower}/\code{Upper}/\code{Status} (an
unavailable negative-variance point can coexist with a valid interval, and
\code{NA} bounds never denote zero uncertainty).

Read G, Phi and measurement SEM together with their confidence intervals,
methods and limitations. Ordinary \code{summary(d)} retains its coefficient-table
return value. Request intervals explicitly, or reuse saved interval results.

Compare future plans on one shared coefficient or SEM axis. Each plan has adjacent
method rows, distinguished by color, point shape and line type. Method
agreement in a single dataset is not evidence of coverage.
}
\details{
\strong{One-facet pivots and plan differences.} For observed facet count \code{k},
future count \code{m}, and \code{R = 1 + k * sigma_P^2 / sigma_E^2}, \code{"exact_f"}
intersects \code{(MS_P / MS_E) / F_quantiles} with \code{R >= 1} before mapping
\code{G_m = m*(R - 1) / (m*(R - 1) + k)} (\code{Status = "Empty parameter-space interval"} if wholly below \code{1}). One-facet \code{"gci"} draws \code{Q_j = df_j * MS_j / X_j} and evaluates Mondal et al. (2025, Eq. 11)
\code{max(0, Q_P - Q_E) / (Q_P + (k/n)*Q_F + (k - 1 - k/n)*Q_E)}, mapping to
\code{Phi_m = m*rho / (1 + (m - 1)*rho)} (\code{attr(ci, "monte_carlo")} stores
binomial order-statistic endpoint bands). With \code{reference} (\code{a} vs \code{b}),
shared-ratio methods map the entire base interval for \code{t} through
\code{h(t) = (b - a)*t / ((1 + a*t)*(1 + b*t))}, including the interior stationary
point \code{t = 1 / sqrt(a*b)}, rather than subtracting marginal endpoints.

\strong{Nested reduction and GENOVA adjustments.} When raters are nested within
tasks and future child counts per parent match the balanced source count \code{R},
cell means reduce \code{G}/\code{Phi} to the one-facet sampling law over parent counts;
changed child counts require explicit \code{"approximate_f"},
\code{"mean_square_region"}, \code{"reml_profile"}, or \code{"delta"}. For GENOVA-adjusted
D-Studies, \code{Estimate} retains the adjusted point while \code{IntervalEstimate} and
bounds target the unadjusted population parameter (\code{ComponentTreatment},
\code{IntervalBasis}); finite-sample interval behavior depends on source degrees of
freedom and rating-plan balance.

The intervals describe uncertainty from the observed G-study in
the population coefficient or SEM of a specified future complete plan.
They do not represent data already collected under that future plan.
G and Phi are dimensionless; SEM is in the selected score's units.
A larger G/Phi or smaller SEM does not alone determine a worthwhile plan:
consider the intended decision, score meaning and task/rater burden.

\code{reference} requests the selected method's shared-source plan differences
for each metric; this is not subtraction of independently constructed
marginal intervals. For a balanced two-facet source, explicitly select
\code{method = "mean_square_region"} for conservative joint-region enclosures.
This summary is not an input to \code{\link[=recommend_mfrm_design]{recommend_mfrm_design()}}, whose D-study
route uses \link{mfrm_d_study_comparison}. Positive
G/Phi differences and negative SEM differences favor the comparison plan,
conditional on the stated model. Individual-plan and difference results
remain explicitly labeled if both are supplied as saved results.

Printing and reopening a saved summary do not calculate intervals or
refit models. Printing separates the main estimates, method qualifications
and rows with unavailable points/intervals or incomplete width formulas;
\code{table} retains all statuses and diagnostics columns. A completed approximate
interval is qualified by its method, not automatically labeled a failure.
Save the complete summary with \code{saveRDS()} to retain its
context; CSV export of \code{table} alone loses weights, notes and diagnostics.
Use \code{plot(s$intervals[[1]])} for its existing forest plot, or supply a
suitable single-metric individual-plan result to \code{plot(d, intervals = ...)}.
A summary does not create simultaneous guarantees across metrics, scores
or selected plans. Read each method's qualifications. Availability is not
proof of model validity or accurate finite-sample coverage.

All methods concern the same score/composite, metric and
source data. A filled point has an interval; an open point is an available
estimate without an interval. Unavailable intervals are explicitly labeled
in the combined view, or marked by an asterisk in the panel view, even
if their point estimates are missing. An interval
can exist without an admissible ANOVA/MINQUE point estimate. Missing values
remain missing; no substitute interval or zero is drawn.
The shared axis includes \verb{[0,1]} for coefficients and \verb{[-1,1]} for G/Phi
differences, expanding to retain all finite estimates and accepted bounds.
Delta normal-reference bounds outside \verb{[0,1]} remain visible; they flag
limitations of that approximation, not a larger population parameter space.
SEM and SEM-difference axes instead span the estimates and intervals in
score units, including zero. Negative SEM differences favor the comparison
plan. An SEM interval is uncertainty in measurement error, not an interval
for a Person's score. Chi-square and MLS labels retain the calculation
used for each plan; their assumptions and coverage claims differ.
Method limitations, confidence level, normal-effect assumptions and
negative components remain in the returned notes and source object.
Plotting neither refits a model nor recalculates an interval. Save the
complete interval or plot object with \code{saveRDS()} for later use.
Nested plan labels show child counts per parent, rather than total child
pool sizes; the reference plan uses the same count convention.
A mean-square-region difference is a conservative enclosure from shared
source uncertainty, not a tight joint image or a paired-delta interval.
Its returned source retains the individual projections and reference.
REML profile and residual-contrast rows retain the original source points;
the separate \code{IntervalCenter} and method diagnostics are available in the source
interval object. A point can lie outside this separately calculated interval.
For GENOVA-adjusted results, points use the selected component treatment;
intervals retain their original-source population basis. \code{IntervalEstimate}
and \code{IntervalBasis} in the source table distinguish them. Drawing the two
together does not make the original sampling SE an adjusted-estimator SE.
Unresolved numerical candidates are retained in the source object but
are not drawn as accepted confidence bounds.
For exact-F, score and MLS intervals, an empty confidence set is labeled
\code{"Empty set"}; it is distinct from an estimated coefficient of zero.
MLS rows with \code{FormulaComplete = FALSE} retain candidate values where
a squared width is undefined. Their conservative bounds are drawn, while
the saved interval object and notes retain the reason and affected ranges.
}
\section{Choose the operation}{

\code{print(d)} displays the D-study and returns it invisibly; \code{summary(d)}
returns its coefficient table. This opt-in summary collects intervals;
\code{confint(d)} calculates one metric at a time with the same inference rules.
Neither changes \code{d}. \code{print(s)} displays the saved summary without any
estimation, and \code{s$table} is the data frame for subsetting or CSV export.
Use \code{plot(d)} for planning curves and \code{plot(s$intervals$Phi)} for a saved
interval forest. A summary list itself is not a new plotting input.

This page explains the interface and reading order. Mathematical
constructions, design-specific assumptions, numerical diagnostics and
references are in \code{\link[=confint.mfrm_multivariate_d_study]{confint.mfrm_multivariate_d_study()}}.
}

\section{Session plot defaults}{

See \link{mfrmr_visual_diagnostics} for \code{options(mfrmr.plot_preset)}, argument
precedence, and appearance when saving or reusing a plot.
}

\examples{
# Long-running workflow; run explicitly when needed.
# example("confint.mfrm_multivariate_d_study", run.dontrun = TRUE)
\dontrun{
tasks <- read.csv(system.file("extdata", "mgenova-table12.csv", package = "mfrmr"))
g <- mfrm_g_study(tasks, c("V", "W"), rater = NULL)
d <- mfrm_d_study(g, data.frame(Tasks = c(6, 12)),
  weights = c(V = .5, W = .5))
ci <- confint(d, assumption = "normal")
ci[, c("Tasks", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper", "Status")]
attr(ci, "notes")
# Optional normal approximation: SE describes the coefficient, not measurement SEM.
delta_ci <- confint(d, method = "delta", assumption = "normal")
delta_ci[, c("Tasks", "Estimate", "SE", "Lower", "Upper", "SEStatus")]
phi_ci <- confint(d, parm = "Phi", assumption = "normal", seed = 1)
phi_ci[, c("Tasks", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper", "Status")]
attr(phi_ci, "monte_carlo")$endpoint_bands
# How much would G improve by changing from six to twelve tasks?
change <- confint(d, parm = "G", reference = 1, assumption = "normal")
change[, c("Tasks", "ReferenceCount", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper", "Status")]
# The same planning question for absolute dependability; coverage is approximate.
phi_change <- confint(d, parm = "Phi", reference = 1, assumption = "normal")
phi_change[, c("Tasks", "ReferenceCount", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper", "Status")]
attr(phi_change, "monte_carlo")$endpoint_bands
# Sampling uncertainty in measurement SEM, rather than in G or Phi.
sem_ci <- confint(d, parm = "AbsoluteSEM", assumption = "normal")
sem_ci[, c("Tasks", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper", "Method", "Status")]
sem_change <- confint(d, parm = "RelativeSEM", reference = 1,
                      assumption = "normal")
sem_change[, c("Tasks", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper", "Status")]
sem_plot <- plot(d, type = "sem", metric = "AbsoluteSEM", intervals = TRUE,
                 assumption = "normal", draw = FALSE)
plot_data(sem_plot, "notes")
# Different raters serve each task; retain three raters per task.
data("mfrmr_example_gtheory_nested", package = "mfrmr")
gn <- mfrm_g_study(mfrmr_example_gtheory_nested,
  c("Content", "Organization"), nesting = c(Rater = "Task"))
dn <- mfrm_d_study(gn,
  data.frame(Raters = 3, Tasks = c(6, 12)))
nested_change <- confint(dn, parm = "G", reference = 1, assumption = "normal")
nested_change[, c("Raters", "Tasks", "ReferenceCount", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper")]
attr(nested_change, "nesting_context")
# Incomplete one-facet ratings: relative SEM without likelihood fitting.
observed <- tasks[(tasks$Person + tasks$Task) \%\% 3 != 0, ]
sparse_g <- mfrm_g_study(observed, "V", rater = NULL, method = "minque0")
sparse_d <- mfrm_d_study(sparse_g, data.frame(Tasks = c(6, 12)))
residual_sem <- confint(sparse_d, parm = "RelativeSEM",
  method = "residual_contrast", assumption = "normal")
residual_sem[, c("Tasks", "Estimate", "IntervalCenter", "Lower", "Upper", "Status")]
residual_change <- confint(sparse_d, parm = "RelativeSEM", reference = 1,
  method = "residual_contrast", assumption = "normal")
residual_change[, c("Tasks", "Estimate", "IntervalCenter", "Lower", "Upper")]
# Relative G also uses Person variation; normal Person effects are required.
spectral_g <- confint(sparse_d, method = "spectral_f", assumption = "normal")
spectral_g[, c("Tasks", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper", "Status")]
spectral_change <- confint(sparse_d, method = "spectral_f", reference = 1,
  assumption = "normal")
spectral_change[, c("Tasks", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper")]
# A separate experimental REML model can address G/Phi or absolute SEM.
if (requireNamespace("lme4", quietly = TRUE) && requireNamespace("minqa", quietly = TRUE)) {
  profile_ci <- confint(sparse_d, method = "reml_profile", assumption = "normal")
  profile_ci[, c("Tasks", "Estimate", "IntervalCenter", "Lower", "Upper", "Validation")]
  attr(profile_ci, "notes")
  # Uncertainty in absolute measurement SEM, in score units.
  profile_sem <- confint(sparse_d, parm = "AbsoluteSEM",
                         method = "reml_profile", assumption = "normal")
  plot(sparse_d, type = "sem", metric = "AbsoluteSEM", intervals = profile_sem,
       draw = FALSE)
  # How much does doubling the task count change G?
  profile_change <- confint(sparse_d, reference = 1,
    method = "reml_profile", assumption = "normal")
  profile_change[, c("Tasks", "Estimate", "IntervalCenter", "Lower", "Upper")]
  attr(profile_change, "base_interval")
  plot(profile_change, draw = FALSE)
}
# Start with the default two-crossed-facet approximation on bundled data.
data("mfrmr_example_gtheory_crossed", package = "mfrmr")
crossed <- mfrm_g_study(mfrmr_example_gtheory_crossed,
  c("Content", "Organization"))
future <- mfrm_d_study(crossed,
  data.frame(Raters = c(2, 4), Tasks = c(6, 12)),
  weights = c(Content = .5, Organization = .5))
crossed_ci <- confint(future, parm = "Phi", assumption = "normal")
crossed_ci[, c("Raters", "Tasks", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper",
               "FormulaComplete", "Status")]
attr(crossed_ci, "notes")
# The score method remains an explicit experimental comparison for G.
score_ci <- confint(future, method = "score", assumption = "normal")
score_ci[, c("Scenario", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper", "Empty", "Status")]
# Vary task counts within each rater count to obtain planning-curve ribbons.
curve_plans <- mfrm_d_study(crossed,
  expand.grid(Raters = c(2, 4), Tasks = c(3, 6, 12)),
  weights = c(Content = .5, Organization = .5))
score_curve <- plot(curve_plans, metric = "G", intervals = TRUE,
  method = "score", assumption = "normal", draw = FALSE)
plot_data(score_curve, "notes")
if (requireNamespace("lpSolve", quietly = TRUE)) {
  comparison <- confint(future, parm = "Phi", assumption = "normal",
    method = c("mls", "approximate_f", "mean_square_region"))
  comparison[, c("Scenario", "Method", "Lower", "Upper", "Status")]
  figure <- plot(comparison, draw = FALSE)
  plot_data(figure, "notes")
  if (requireNamespace("ggplot2", quietly = TRUE)) as_ggplot(figure)
  # General nested comparison: both counts change, with eighteen ratings.
  general_nested <- mfrm_d_study(gn,
    data.frame(Raters = c(3, 2, 6), Tasks = c(6, 9, 3)))
  nested_region <- confint(general_nested, parm = "Phi", reference = 1,
    method = "mean_square_region", assumption = "normal")
  nested_region[, c("Raters", "Tasks", "Estimate", "Lower", "Upper", "Status")]
  attr(nested_region, "reference")$counts
}
}
# Optional workflow; run the example explicitly as shown below.
# example("summary.mfrm_multivariate_d_study", run.dontrun = TRUE)
\dontrun{
# Published synthetic data, not empirical speech-contest records.
ratings <- read.csv(system.file("extdata", "mgenova-table12.csv", package = "mfrmr"))
g <- mfrm_g_study(ratings, c("V", "W"), rater = NULL)
d <- mfrm_d_study(g, data.frame(Tasks = c(6, 12)),
  weights = c(V = .5, W = .5))
# Compare dependability and measurement error under the normal-effects model.
s <- summary(d, intervals = TRUE, assumption = "normal")
print(s, digits = 3)
# One row per plan for absolute dependability; the complete table is retained.
subset(s$table, Metric == "Phi",
  select = c(Tasks, Estimate, Lower, Upper, Status))
# Reuse an interval for a curve, with no interval calculation.
p <- plot(d, metric = "Phi", intervals = s$intervals$Phi, draw = FALSE)
plot_data(p, "notes")
}
